        <body>
            <div xml:id="d343">

                <pb facs="0791" n="695" sameAs="frus:frus1879/pg695"/>

                <head>No. 314.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Bingham to Mr. Evarts.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >United States Legation</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Tokei,
                                Japan</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1879-10-09"
                                ><hi rendition="#italic">October</hi> 9, 1879</date>. (Received
                        November 4.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 987.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to my No. 961, wherein I reported
                    the order of this government making the silver yen a legal tender, I now have
                    the honor <pb facs="0792" n="696" xml:id="pg696"/> to further acquaint you that
                    the same is being produced at the imperial mint of Japan at the rate of 30,000
                    per day, and that it is rapidly expelling from trade the Mexican dollar, so long
                    the currency of this empire.</p>
                <p>As the gold yen is also legal tender, this government has established the
                    bimetallic standard in Japan.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>JNO. A. BINGHAM.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
